#!/usr/bin/env python3
# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# ncz-screensaver-feed - the producer half of the NCZ lock-hosted screensaver.
#
# While the session is locked, ext-session-lock-v1 presents exactly one client's
# surface, so a screensaver process cannot draw underneath the lock. The NCZ
# lock client (built with -Dlock_framesrc=enabled) instead imports a buffer file
# descriptor that a separate process sends over an AF_UNIX SOCK_SEQPACKET
# socket, and presents it on its own per-output lock surface. This program is
# that separate process.
#
# It owns the lock's frame-source socket (SINGULARITY_LOCK_RENDERER_SOCKET) as
# the "saver" side of the protocol, and for each lock output runs ONE hack
# binary in off-screen mode (src/ncz_feed_render.c; selected by NCZ_FEED_SOCKET)
# as a child. It relays the child's frames to the lock, one buffer in flight,
# exactly as lock_framesrc_testsrc.c would.
#
# Everything about WHICH hack runs and HOW it is configured is reused from the
# ncz-screensaver launcher (catalog, mode one|random|playlist, pool, per-hack
# options, GPU offload, render scale). This file does not re-implement any of
# that: it loads the launcher module and calls its functions.
#
# Failure containment: this process is arbitrary third-party-adjacent code from
# the lock's point of view. If it crashes, hangs or sends garbage, the lock
# drops back to its own prompt and stays locked. The feed never signals the
# lock; it only ever exits.
#
# Wire format: the shared, dependency-free lock_framesrc_proto.h (installed next
# to this script as lock_framesrc_proto.h; constants below mirror it exactly).

